I realize that it's bad net.form to follow-up your own posting, but I had
a good idea about this that could fix both 'problems.'

In article <11623@netnews.upenn.edu> barron@eniac.seas.upenn.edu.UUCP (Daniel P. Barron) writes:
>In article <T36273C.89Jun2025424@saha.saha.hut.FI> t36273c@saha.saha.hut.FI (Ari Huttunen) writes:

[Stuff about putting graves in nethack deleted]

>This is exactly what my question would be.  I think ghosts and their hoardes
>of [cursed] treasure pretty much foot the bill.  Perhaps you might find a
>corpse near the ghosts sometime, but this would be for effect only, not for
>any good reason, and so is probably unecessary.  
>
>What I think we could use is some other mechanism for raising luck points.

How about this: there is a [small] chance that you may find a dead human near
"Orac's ghost."  If you do, and you have a wand of undead turning, you can
raise him from the dead.  This could a) make him eternally grateful and thus
give you a "tame" human slave/partner, or b) he simply thanks you, leaves
and uncurses all of his stuff that's there, and goes away, or c) he takes all
of his stuff (unless you fight him) but the gods regard this as a *very* good
deed, your luck maxes out, you get the "golden-glow" or some other benefit.

The "tame human" bit sounds like a major rewrite, that is a very large
can of worms you'd be opening.  How would you share equipment, food, etc?
But option (c) sounds feasible.

What does the net think?

